                        "Information": [
                            {
                                "ReferenceNumber": 1,
                                "Value": {
                                    "StringWithMarkup": [
                                        {
                                            "String": "Ginsenosides or panaxosides are a class of steroid glycosides and triterpene saponins found exclusively in the plant genus TAX-4053 (ginseng). They are the principal components responsible for the pharmaceutical activities of ginseng.\n\nGinsenosides are composed of an aglycone and sugar moieties. Based on the structure of their aglycones, ginsenosides have been categorized into three broad types; Protopanaxadiol-type-ginsenosides (PPD), which contain hydroxyl groups at positions 3 and 20, Protopanaxatriol-type-ginsenosides (PPT), which contain hydroxyl groups at positions 3, 6 and 20, and Oleanonic-acid-type-ginsenosides.\n\nAlthough ginsenosides only have three kinds of aglycones, more than 60 kinds of ginsenosides have been identified by now due to the variability in the type of sugar moieties, their number, and the carbon positions they link to.\n\nThe pharmacological activities of various ginsenosides include cardio-protective, immuno-modulatory, anti-fatigue, antitumour and hepato-protective effects [Yue05]. Ginsenosides are produced in the roots of TAX-4054 [LUO05], and various types of ginsenosides accumulate as the plant ages [PMID: 15356323].\n\nThe different ginsenoside backbones are synthesized by different forms of cyclization of EPOXYSQUALENE, catalyzed by specific enzymes [PMID: 15356323]. The triterpene backbones then undergo various modifications, such as oxidation, substitution, and glycosylation to give the characteristic ginsenosides.\n\nThe chemical synthesis of ginsenosides has not been feasible. Recent discoveries of the enzymes involved lead the way to biosynthesis using recombinant technology [PMID: 26032089].",
                                            "Markup": [
